{"objects":[{"id":"flow","instance":"instance--b96dad5f-c519-5a1f-a718-912ca4a86c02","objects":["attack-action--1","attack-action--2","attack-action--3","attack-action--4","software--1","instance--f64caa83-12dd-5204-a53f-519db6b69a9d","instance--3536a568-610f-5570-bba9-6dfe97bc9c2e","instance--384798bd-a84b-5feb-8d38-7f9d0a087f11","instance--ed6039fe-678b-5ae9-9576-73e6ea35b0c8"],"properties":[["name","Sparse attacker behavior references from extracted Trend Micro page"],["description","The extracted page is largely unrelated site content, but it contains brief source-grounded references to credential theft, phishing abuse of device code authentication, fake sites exploiting the 2026 FIFA World Cup, and an autonomous ransomware intrusion."],["scope","malware"],["author",[["name","Unknown"]]],["external_references",[["item-1",[["source_name","blog.trendmicro.com"],["url","http://blog.trendmicro.com/trendlabs-security-intelligence/rarstone-found-in-targeted-attacks/"]]]]]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--ad592d05-6969-5b51-98a8-1ad9b6aab896","120":"instance--5850a8f4-8241-5e73-9bf0-dcd376cd5025","150":"instance--0da1498f-3beb-5916-a249-e0410a4a81e4","180":"instance--d3632784-7391-5a29-83e2-edab51db56c6","210":"instance--8bc47ca5-ca73-5a04-a9ea-918608f10871","240":"instance--6546eef6-c5ad-516e-a417-89916e651648","270":"instance--244e5a69-f465-564e-9d2c-93d4da44fc57","30":"instance--6a1fca3c-fdb6-536a-bbea-61ac2349a700","300":"instance--043a5b44-2864-5565-abce-1909204e5485","330":"instance--e10eb918-2b0b-56f4-9c82-f345ce21276c","60":"instance--33a99d1b-dad8-51f6-be74-e1614db9151b","90":"instance--380c79e9-b1eb-547c-bf01-a011db1201c1"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--1","properties":[["name","Steal Microsoft 365 credentials"],["description","Kratos, the phishing-as-a-Service (PhaaS) platform behind a large share of recent Microsoft 365 credential theft, has been taken offline by the BKA and ZIT in an operation dubbed Olympus Blade."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0006","technique":"T1056"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--4f4d03a8-4502-5010-997b-9943e254395b","120":"instance--cefe62ac-5b6e-5ead-84fc-b4c632913a73","150":"instance--4d07a740-2467-5b95-ac8d-4a2ee8edd11e","180":"instance--1205f03c-a457-5a0b-87ef-3bbb7aac110f","210":"instance--96e7dc6a-9f86-5f69-8d45-a566303cd4c0","240":"instance--66237af6-bb19-5a51-9fa2-cbc4a96186f5","270":"instance--836dfd42-b475-50ca-9acf-4f11bd32b89f","30":"instance--efe92997-cc5c-538a-90bc-c09ffb1379f4","300":"instance--9c4a24ae-1426-5cb5-9d7c-792e6dd325b7","330":"instance--06c6a74a-ca7d-5703-88ea-a06a0933d21a","60":"instance--5e4f84e5-56b7-5b8f-9bc3-b4d59d371123","90":"instance--019deb6a-bab3-57db-9a53-11e3564bf7a4"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--2","properties":[["name","Exploit fake World Cup sites"],["description","Between January and June 2026, TrendAI\u2122 tracked more than 35,000 fake sites exploiting the 2026 FIFA World Cup, spanning counterfeit merchandise shops, cloned ticket pages, and bogus free-streaming sites, which together drew roughly 1.48 million visits from Japan."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0042","technique":"T1583.001"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0ce35aea-35a2-52ae-a67b-985306bdefe7","120":"instance--312639d5-e6d9-57b1-850d-28f0a1eac2f6","150":"instance--9551a25e-2558-512e-9894-dd64a8d662bf","180":"instance--403becce-e0ad-5dcd-bc14-7c00453907da","210":"instance--2305f501-2712-5ec6-9d4e-1371b011e937","240":"instance--cf1b8cf6-1cbd-5afb-b653-aa693889dab5","270":"instance--1d3dc59a-40a0-5670-aa3e-1304ff762003","30":"instance--77063d4f-503a-5e9b-b622-15aa2cc6a8e7","300":"instance--beaab67f-8a54-5982-8c5d-9d2ed2d42c9f","330":"instance--4d3274ff-ef14-5a53-99be-01575194a98e","60":"instance--4154a6bf-290b-5fce-a557-5dc0f3e700fb","90":"instance--2992bc0b-df57-54e5-a17b-03a964338c09"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--3","properties":[["name","Run ransomware intrusion autonomously"],["description","An AI agent has run a ransomware intrusion on its own for the first time, from break-in to data destruction."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0040","technique":"T1485"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0f6e4da7-a5f4-54d7-866f-3bc6e8bcb019","120":"instance--015254b5-0906-577e-b3e3-3899f837d118","150":"instance--f7af5b8c-330a-58d5-b0da-a3d5f0bc6ad0","180":"instance--9f27ab21-2382-567d-b022-74ad5a29bd08","210":"instance--d799eb0a-7600-5b1a-a298-c47a78ceae4b","240":"instance--911c2c52-5974-5e08-b0b6-a9084d57c6ee","270":"instance--286444b4-97a1-58cd-9ca3-cffa04a15351","30":"instance--92192c38-5143-50e0-8c39-a96ea86e32e2","300":"instance--82bb3de0-bea5-5651-a1de-583283258c6d","330":"instance--4f0945c8-f644-50ce-8796-fea52a29dda1","60":"instance--16c43a2d-7356-521d-a507-5922e2c3337b","90":"instance--abce5c62-339a-5ed5-a0b8-dd66136cfe2f"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--4","properties":[["name","Abuse device code phishing"],["description","Device code phishing abuses a legitimate authentication feature designed for devices with limited input capabilities. This article breaks down how the technique works, examines a recent observed case, and outlines the layered security measures organizations can implement."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0001","technique":"T1566"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--905f37ff-524a-522a-8685-08b6df9b34bd","120":"instance--a0b8bf42-8310-53d9-87f0-390378638ae9","150":"instance--ffafa2fa-6430-5714-9c5e-bbf977c9819b","180":"instance--10bf0e4d-ac4d-5742-b82b-398ef2467e2a","210":"instance--f2066b53-6fa4-5efa-8f61-dc81d3df5b0e","240":"instance--fbb6b151-c9f4-5cb6-b467-c563cc0c60d3","270":"instance--451ff5e8-5cc8-5d6c-b701-da17c04936f7","30":"instance--059bc992-da53-5d4b-ac82-60cfacc08e81","300":"instance--1d241382-a8ee-554a-aa3a-04366535b315","330":"instance--8a7078e9-787d-56b2-a734-f93cd76b3dc8","60":"instance--8aab1831-f6e3-582d-8f9b-3ad920a40e35","90":"instance--192458e5-76c8-5d8c-ab54-9ed8b36ef2bd"},"id":"software","instance":"software--1","properties":[["evidence",[["ite